    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "heights = [[1,2,2],[3,8,2],[5,3,5]]                       ->  2\nheights = [[1,2,3],[3,8,4],[5,3,5]]                       ->  1   (along the top and down the right)\nheights = [[1,2,1,1,1],[1,2,1,2,1],[1,2,1,2,1],[1,1,1,2,1]]   ->  0   (a perfectly flat winding road)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: Dijkstra with a different cost"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Cells are vertices and neighbouring cells are joined by edges weighted by their height difference. But a route's cost isn't the **sum** of its edges — it's the **largest** one. Dijkstra still works, because extending a route can never make its cost go **down**: the cost of a route through `u` to `v` is `max(effort to u, |height difference|)`, which is at least the effort to `u`."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "So run Dijkstra unchanged except for one line: replace `d + w` with `max(e, step)`. The heap hands back the cell with the easiest route so far; when the bottom-right cell comes out, its effort is the answer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There's also a second way in. The answer is **monotone**: if you can cross with effort 5, you can cross with any limit above 5. Questions like that can be **binary searched** — guess a limit, check it with a plain BFS that only takes steps within the limit, and narrow down."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Binary search on the answer"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Binary search the smallest limit for which a BFS, allowed only steps of at most that limit, reaches the bottom-right."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const DIRS = [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]];\n\nfunction minimumEffortPath(heights) {\n  const rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;\n  const canCross = (limit) => {             // a plain BFS using only steps of at most limit\n    const seen = heights.map((row) => row.map(() => false));\n    seen[0][0] = true;\n    const queue = [[0, 0]];\n    for (let head = 0; head < queue.length; head++) {\n      const [r, c] = queue[head];\n      if (r === rows - 1 && c === cols - 1) return true;\n      for (const [dr, dc] of DIRS) {\n        const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;\n        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && !seen[nr][nc] &&\n            Math.abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]) <= limit) {\n          seen[nr][nc] = true;\n          queue.push([nr, nc]);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    return false;\n  };\n  let lo = 0, hi = 1000000;                 // heights are between 1 and 10^6\n  while (lo < hi) {                         // the smallest limit that works\n    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);\n    if (canCross(mid)) hi = mid;\n    else lo = mid + 1;\n  }\n  return lo;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from collections import deque\n\nDIRS = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n\ndef minimum_effort_path(heights):\n    rows, cols = len(heights), len(heights[0])\n\n    def can_cross(limit):                 # a plain BFS using only steps of at most limit\n        seen = [[False] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n        seen[0][0] = True\n        queue = deque([(0, 0)])\n        while queue:\n            r, c = queue.popleft()\n            if r == rows - 1 and c == cols - 1:\n                return True\n            for dr, dc in DIRS:\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if (0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols and not seen[nr][nc]\n                        and abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]) <= limit):\n                    seen[nr][nc] = True\n                    queue.append((nr, nc))\n        return False\n\n    lo, hi = 0, 10**6                     # heights are between 1 and 10^6\n    while lo < hi:                        # the smallest limit that works\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if can_cross(mid):\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "static final int[][] DIRS = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n\nint minimumEffortPath(int[][] heights) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = 1000000;                    // heights are between 1 and 10^6\n    while (lo < hi) {                            // the smallest limit that works\n        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;\n        if (canCross(heights, mid)) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nboolean canCross(int[][] heights, int limit) {   // a plain BFS using only steps of at most limit\n    int rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;\n    boolean[][] seen = new boolean[rows][cols];\n    seen[0][0] = true;\n    ArrayDeque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    queue.add(new int[]{0, 0});\n    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] cur = queue.poll();\n        int r = cur[0], c = cur[1];\n        if (r == rows - 1 && c == cols - 1) return true;\n        for (int[] d : DIRS) {\n            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && !seen[nr][nc]\n                    && Math.abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]) <= limit) {\n                seen[nr][nc] = true;\n                queue.add(new int[]{nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "const int DIRS[4][2] = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n\nbool canCross(vector<vector<int>>& heights, int limit) {   // a plain BFS using only steps of at most limit\n    int rows = heights.size(), cols = heights[0].size();\n    vector<vector<bool>> seen(rows, vector<bool>(cols, false));\n    seen[0][0] = true;\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    q.push({0, 0});\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [r, c] = q.front(); q.pop();\n        if (r == rows - 1 && c == cols - 1) return true;\n        for (auto& d : DIRS) {\n            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && !seen[nr][nc]\n                    && abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]) <= limit) {\n                seen[nr][nc] = true;\n                q.push({nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nint minimumEffortPath(vector<vector<int>>& heights) {\n    int lo = 0, hi = 1000000;                    // heights are between 1 and 10^6\n    while (lo < hi) {                            // the smallest limit that works\n        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;\n        if (canCross(heights, mid)) hi = mid;\n        else lo = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return lo;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(m · n · log H), space O(m · n).** H is the range of heights (10⁶ here), so about 20 BFS runs over the m · n cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Dijkstra, where a route costs its biggest step"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Keep the best effort found for every cell, and a min-heap of `[effort, row, col]`. Pop the easiest cell, skip it if out of date, stop if it's the target, and otherwise offer each neighbour `max(effort, step)`."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "min-effort-path",
      "caption": "The easiest unsettled cell always comes out of the heap next. Try the map with the peak in the middle."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const DIRS = [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]];\n\nfunction minimumEffortPath(heights) {\n  const rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;\n  const effort = heights.map((row) => row.map(() => Infinity));   // best effort to reach each cell\n  effort[0][0] = 0;\n  const heap = new Heap((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);   // [effort, row, col], easiest first\n  heap.push([0, 0, 0]);\n  while (heap.size() > 0) {\n    const [e, r, c] = heap.pop();\n    if (e > effort[r][c]) continue;         // a stale entry\n    if (r === rows - 1 && c === cols - 1) return e;   // settled: no easier path exists\n    for (const [dr, dc] of DIRS) {\n      const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;\n      if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) continue;\n      const next = Math.max(e, Math.abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]));   // a path costs its biggest step\n      if (next < effort[nr][nc]) {\n        effort[nr][nc] = next;\n        heap.push([next, nr, nc]);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return 0;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "import heapq\n\nDIRS = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n\ndef minimum_effort_path(heights):\n    rows, cols = len(heights), len(heights[0])\n    effort = [[float(\"inf\")] * cols for _ in range(rows)]   # best effort to reach each cell\n    effort[0][0] = 0\n    heap = [(0, 0, 0)]                    # (effort, row, col): the easiest comes out first\n    while heap:\n        e, r, c = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if e > effort[r][c]:\n            continue                      # a stale entry\n        if r == rows - 1 and c == cols - 1:\n            return e                      # settled: no easier path exists\n        for dr, dc in DIRS:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                nxt = max(e, abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]))   # a path costs its biggest step\n                if nxt < effort[nr][nc]:\n                    effort[nr][nc] = nxt\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (nxt, nr, nc))\n    return 0"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "static final int[][] DIRS = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n\nint minimumEffortPath(int[][] heights) {\n    int rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;\n    int[][] effort = new int[rows][cols];                // best effort to reach each cell\n    for (int[] row : effort) Arrays.fill(row, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n    effort[0][0] = 0;\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));   // [effort, row, col]\n    heap.add(new int[]{0, 0, 0});\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] top = heap.poll();\n        int e = top[0], r = top[1], c = top[2];\n        if (e > effort[r][c]) continue;                  // a stale entry\n        if (r == rows - 1 && c == cols - 1) return e;    // settled: no easier path exists\n        for (int[] d : DIRS) {\n            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n            if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) continue;\n            int next = Math.max(e, Math.abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]));   // a path costs its biggest step\n            if (next < effort[nr][nc]) {\n                effort[nr][nc] = next;\n                heap.add(new int[]{next, nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "const int DIRS[4][2] = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n\nint minimumEffortPath(vector<vector<int>>& heights) {\n    int rows = heights.size(), cols = heights[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> effort(rows, vector<int>(cols, INT_MAX));   // best effort to reach each cell\n    effort[0][0] = 0;\n    priority_queue<array<int, 3>, vector<array<int, 3>>, greater<>> heap;   // {effort, row, col}, easiest first\n    heap.push({0, 0, 0});\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto [e, r, c] = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n        if (e > effort[r][c]) continue;                  // a stale entry\n        if (r == rows - 1 && c == cols - 1) return e;    // settled: no easier path exists\n        for (auto& d : DIRS) {\n            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n            if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) continue;\n            int next = max(e, abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]));   // a path costs its biggest step\n            if (next < effort[nr][nc]) {\n                effort[nr][nc] = next;\n                heap.push({next, nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(m · n · log(m · n)), space O(m · n).** Each cell has at most 4 edges, so the heap sees O(m · n) pushes and pops at a logarithmic cost each. It stops as soon as the target is settled."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Binary search on the answer",
          "time": "O(m · n · log H)",
          "timeWhy": "About **log H** guesses (H = height range), each one **BFS** over the m · n cells.",
          "space": "O(m · n)",
          "spaceWhy": "One BFS's visited grid and queue."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Dijkstra, where a route costs its biggest step",
          "time": "O(m · n · log(m · n))",
          "timeWhy": "Each cell has **4 edges**, so O(m · n) heap operations at **log** cost each.",
          "space": "O(m · n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The effort grid and the **heap**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Both are accepted and similarly fast in practice. Dijkstra is the pattern to know; binary search on the answer is a useful second tool whenever \"is X enough?\" is easy to check and gets easier as X grows."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**A single cell** — no steps at all: effort 0.",
        "**A flat grid** — every step is 0, so the answer is 0 however winding the route.",
        "**The direct route is worse** — the best route can go the long way round to avoid one steep step.",
        "**Stopping early** — return when the target is **popped**, not when it's first pushed: a later, easier route may still improve it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
